module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.pending_run_cancelled_by_a_later_dispatch_with_no_signal

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data pending_run_cancelled_by_a_later_dispatch_with_no_signal: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "pending_run_cancelled_by_a_later_dispatch_with_no_signal" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "**a workflow serializes its runs with a bare concurrency group, so the platform keeps ONE pending run per group and cancels the one already waiting when another is dispatched -- and the lane that dispatched the cancelled run is told nothing.** INVALID STATE: a dispatched run is a receipt someone is waiting on; under `concurrency: <group>` its pending form can be replaced by any later dispatch from any lane. HARM: the evidence a change is waiting for never exists, and the wait is indistinguishable from a slow queue until someone reads the run list. The remedy that suggests itself, dispatching again, does the same to whoever is now pending. DISTINGUISHING FACTS: the cancelled run has conclusion `cancelled` and ZERO jobs (it never started), which separates it from a run cancelled mid-flight; the group has exactly one in-progress run and the superseding run is the pending one.",
    "RECEIPT (2026-10-03): instrument-dispatch run 37114501769 (self-host behavioral-equivalence, queued 09:52Z behind the in-progress run 37112143403) was cancelled with no jobs when another lane dispatched a different instrument label at 11:14Z (run 37119024146). The workflow's group was the scalar `instrument-dispatch`. Its owner found the cancellation by listing the workflow's runs.",
    "PRIOR ART, WHICH IS WHY THE REPAIR IS A ROW AND NOT A MECHANISM: extdeps.github.actions ConcurrencyMappingQueueMax already models GitHub's `queue: max`, grounded by the measurement recorded in the note on gunbc.fleet_desired_admission_workflow fleet_desired_admission_workflow (with the key all four runs completed; without it two of four were cancelled while pending), and gunbc.heal_publisher_workflow adopted it for the stated reason that a cancelled pending entry is a repair silently lost. The dispatch workflow was authored after both and used the scalar form.",
    "REPAIR: gunbc.instrument_dispatch_workflow instrument_dispatch_concurrency is ConcurrencyMappingQueueMax with cancel-in-progress false, on ONE group for every label, so dispatches still run one at a time on the shared pool and none is dropped. Per-label groups were considered and not taken: they would let two instruments run at once, which the workflow's own operator condition (queue rather than stack) forbids.",
    "SECOND RECEIPT, WHICH FALSIFIED THE REPAIR AS FIRST WRITTEN (2026-10-03): with the queue-max form on the SAME group name, three runs dispatched back-to-back (37121808798, 37121810538, 37121812254) were pending together from 12:06Z to 13:34Z -- so queue-max dispatches do not cancel one another -- and were then all cancelled within three seconds, zero jobs each, by run 37126626593, dispatched from the default branch, which still carried the scalar form. Concurrency is read from the workflow at the DISPATCHING ref, and one scalar-form dispatch cancels EVERY pending run in its group whatever form those runs carry. A form change alone therefore protects a queue only from refs that already have it. REPAIR, SECOND HALF: instrument_dispatch_concurrency_group names a group no pre-fix ref names, so a pre-fix dispatch cannot reach this queue. COST, BOUNDED AND SELF-DISSOLVING: while pre-fix refs still dispatch, one old-form run may execute alongside one run from this queue; it ends when no pre-fix ref dispatches.",
    "GENERAL FORM OF THE LIMIT: any change to a workflow's concurrency semantics takes effect per ref, so until every dispatching ref carries it the old semantics remain reachable in a shared group; changing the group name is what makes the cut atomic for the new refs. RUNG AND CEILING: mechanically preventable, by the witness test.claim.instruments.instrument_dispatch_workflow_witness_test asserting the queue-max mapping in the generated text. Not structural: the scalar form remains writable for any workflow, and `queue` is an undocumented key whose behaviour is grounded by observation (see the note on ConcurrencySpec), with a pending ceiling read from prose. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: a workflow-level rule that a dispatch-triggered workflow carrying a concurrency group must use the queue-max form or declare why dropping a pending run is acceptable, SUFFICIENT FOR the scalar form on a dispatch-only workflow to refuse at generation.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.instrument_dispatch_workflow", decl_name: "instrument_dispatch_concurrency", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "extdeps.github.actions", decl_name: "ConcurrencySpec", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
